<body>
  <div class="title">
    <h1>Quaerens Ltd</h1>
    <h2>Revised 12-Month P&amp;L and Operating Forecast</h2>
    <p class="note">Prepared for discussion with prospective legal partners</p>
  </div>

  <div class="box">
    <strong>Purpose of this document:</strong> This document explains Quaerens' current operating position, existing claim channels, cost base, lead-generation capacity and expected growth if a legal partner enables a no-win-no-fee route for suitable clients.
  </div>

  <h1>1. Executive Summary</h1>
  <p>Quaerens Ltd has built a consumer-facing claims support platform with live websites, lead-generation campaigns, telephone intake, administration support, document preparation and client correspondence workflows.</p>
  <p>Since launch, the main operational focus has been mis-sold solar panel enquiries. Quaerens has been successful in generating and converting solar-related enquiries, but the market has also been heavily approached by other claims companies. A number of potential clients had already started procedures elsewhere and had lost confidence due to poor communication, limited transparency and unclear back-office progress from those providers.</p>
  <p>Despite that market friction, the level of client interest remains strong. Based on telephone conversations and previous campaign performance, Quaerens believes a significantly higher proportion of suitable clients can be converted where the route is offered on a no-win-no-fee basis through a regulated legal partner.</p>

  <h1>2. Current Operating Model</h1>
  <p>At present, Quaerens operates an intake-fee and mediation-style support model. Clients are charged an upfront intake fee and enter into a mediation contract with an end success charge of a minimum of 25% where successful, with the initial intake fee deducted from the final charge.</p>
  <p>This model has allowed Quaerens to test client appetite, lead quality and conversion rates without pushing advertising at full scale. It has also helped confirm which claim categories produce meaningful enquiries and which operational steps are needed before a case can be handed to a law firm.</p>

  <h2>Current Back-Office Process</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Initial client contact and telephone intake.</li>
    <li>Collection of case information and supporting documents.</li>
    <li>Preparation of letters, correspondence and case summaries.</li>
    <li>Sending correspondence to responsible parties using information supplied by the client.</li>
    <li>Client updates and administration support.</li>
    <li>Preparation of an organised handover pack where a legal route is available.</li>
  </ul>

  <h1>3. Solar Panel Claims Experience</h1>
  <p>The initial core focus was mis-sold solar panels. Quaerens targets clients who purchased or financed solar panels using finance agreements, debit card payments, credit card payments or instalment arrangements.</p>
  <p>The common claim basis is that the solar installer has entered administration or ceased trading, leaving the customer without the guarantee, service support or protection they expected. Many clients are still paying for systems or finance arrangements while no longer having practical recourse against the installer.</p>
  <p>Quaerens' experience suggests that solar remains a viable lead source, but the conversion rate under an upfront-fee model is lower than it would be under a no-win-no-fee model because many clients have already been approached by competitors and are cautious about paying further upfront fees.</p>

  <div class="box">
    <strong>Expected solar potential under a legal partner model:</strong> Based on previous conversations and campaign performance, Quaerens believes a no-win-no-fee route could support approximately 15 to 30 converted solar clients per week, subject to legal partner acceptance criteria and case screening.
  </div>

  <h1>4. Spray Foam Claims Experience</h1>
  <p>Quaerens moved into spray foam insulation enquiries after identifying that the solar market had become highly competitive and, in some areas, damaged by poor consumer experience with other claims companies.</p>
  <p>Spray foam campaigns are currently run without advertising a no-win-no-fee route and continue to generate approximately 25 to 30 leads per week. Current conversion from generated internet campaign leads is around 25% under the upfront-fee model.</p>
  <p>During the initial spray foam campaign test, Quaerens generated 178 spray foam leads. This provided a useful basis for assessing demand, lead quality, likely client objections and the operational requirements needed before scaling the campaign further.</p>
  <p>When campaigns previously advertised a no-win-no-fee route, Quaerens generated around 10 leads per day. However, without a legal partner in place to support a no-win-no-fee pathway, only a smaller proportion of those enquiries could be converted into paying clients.</p>

  <div class="box">
    <strong>Expected spray foam potential under a legal partner model:</strong> If Quaerens can return to clear no-win-no-fee advertising with a legal partner, management expects lead volume to return to previous levels and conversion to improve materially. The target conversion range for suitable leads is approximately 75% to 85%, subject to eligibility, evidence and legal partner criteria.
  </div>

  <h1>5. Additional Claim Routes Ready to Launch</h1>
  <p>Quaerens has prepared additional consumer claim routes which can be activated or scaled once a legal partner is in place. These routes are intended to diversify lead sources and reduce reliance on one single market.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Claim Area</th><th>Status / Opportunity</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Caravan and Holiday Park Mis-selling</td><td>Consumer-facing route prepared; suitable for clients affected by unfair park sales, finance, pitch-fee or ownership issues.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Car Finance Claims</td><td>High-volume consumer market; can be supported by existing lead capture and intake workflow.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Equity Release Issues</td><td>New hub and topic pages prepared; potential issues include unsuitable advice, inheritance impact, compound interest, investments and property schemes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sale &amp; Rent Back</td><td>Dedicated topic route prepared; lower-competition market with potentially significant consumer losses.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Spray Foam Insulation</td><td>Active lead-generation route with proven interest and existing conversion data.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Solar Panel Claims</td><td>Established experience and existing client conversations; likely stronger where no-win-no-fee legal route is available.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h1>6. Why Quaerens Has Not Yet Pushed Full Volume</h1>
  <p>Quaerens has intentionally not pushed campaigns as aggressively as possible because management wanted to establish realistic conversion data under the current model and avoid creating volumes that could not be progressed properly without a legal partner.</p>
  <p>The infrastructure is already in place to support higher volumes, but the business has deliberately tested lead quality, client willingness and back-office capacity before scaling. The current limitation is not lead demand but the funding and progression route offered to clients.</p>

  <h1>7. Lead Cost and Conversion Position</h1>
  <p>Because Quaerens is currently unable to advertise a confirmed no-win-no-fee legal route across all campaigns, lead costs are higher than management expects under a legal-partner model.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Campaign Position</th><th>Current / Expected Impact</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Current upfront-fee model</td><td>Average lead cost is approximately £70 to £80 per generated lead.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>No-win-no-fee advertising available</td><td>Expected to materially reduce lead costs and improve consumer trust and conversion.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Spray foam campaign test</td><td>178 leads generated during the test period, giving Quaerens practical data on demand, objections and case-screening requirements.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Current spray foam lead volume</td><td>Approximately 25 to 30 leads per week without no-win-no-fee messaging.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Previous no-win-no-fee spray foam campaigns</td><td>Approximately 10 leads per day.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Expected solar conversion under partner model</td><td>Approximately 15 to 30 converted solar clients per week, subject to legal review criteria.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h1>8. Current Monthly Cost Base</h1>
  <p>The following monthly cost base reflects the existing structure required to generate enquiries, manage campaigns, handle clients and prepare documentation. Costs are expected to increase after collaboration begins, particularly in administration and agent support, because higher lead volumes will require additional staffing.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Cost Item</th><th>Current Monthly Cost</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Office premises rent</td><td>£800</td><td>Operating premises.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Director accommodation / support cost</td><td>£1,200</td><td>Current fixed monthly support cost.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Administration department</td><td>£2,000</td><td>Expected to increase to approximately £4,000 as volumes grow.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Agents / intake support</td><td>£2,000</td><td>Expected to increase as lead generation scales.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Campaign management - in house</td><td>£2,000</td><td>Internal campaign management.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AI subscriptions and software tools</td><td>£400</td><td>Operational subscriptions and AI tooling.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Development and technical support</td><td>£1,200</td><td>Website, systems and technical support.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Telephone costs</td><td>£400</td><td>Call handling and communications.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hardware</td><td>£200</td><td>Equipment and related hardware costs.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Office management</td><td>£3,500</td><td>Management and operational oversight.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Miscellaneous operating costs</td><td>£2,500</td><td>General business costs, ad hoc support, travel, subscriptions and other monthly operating expenses.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Total Current Monthly Cost Base</strong></td><td><strong>£16,200</strong></td><td><strong>Equivalent to approximately £194,400 annually.</strong></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h1>9. Collaboration Growth Cost Expectation</h1>
  <p>Following collaboration with a law firm, Quaerens expects administration and agent capacity to increase to handle additional evidence collection, client onboarding, document preparation and law-firm handover requirements.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Cost Area</th><th>Current Monthly Cost</th><th>Expected Collaboration Cost</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Administration department</td><td>£2,000</td><td>Approximately £4,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Agents / intake support</td><td>£2,000</td><td>Expected to increase as campaigns scale</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Other fixed overheads</td><td>£12,200</td><td>Expected to remain broadly stable initially</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The exact growth budget should be agreed after the law firm confirms preferred case categories, acceptance criteria, evidence requirements and expected handover format.</p>

  <h1>10. Revenue Model</h1>
  <p>The current client model includes an upfront intake fee and a minimum 25% success charge where successful, with the intake fee deducted from the final charge.</p>
  <p>Under a legal-partner collaboration, Quaerens would prefer to grow into a no-win-no-fee market because this is likely to increase client trust, reduce friction at intake and allow higher conversion across suitable claims. The final commercial arrangement should be agreed with the law firm and checked for regulatory compliance.</p>
  <div class="warning">
    <strong>Revenue figures intentionally limited:</strong> This forecast does not overstate revenue because the final economics depend on legal partner acceptance criteria, permitted fee structure, regulatory position, case quality and agreed commercial terms.
  </div>

  <h1>11. Proposed Law Firm Handover Model</h1>
  <p>Quaerens can provide the law firm with a structured client pack rather than unfiltered leads. The proposed handover would include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Client contact details and consent records.</li>
    <li>Claim category and basic chronology.</li>
    <li>Financial product or property issue summary.</li>
    <li>Key documents and correspondence collected from the client.</li>
    <li>Initial screening notes and evidence gaps.</li>
    <li>Client expectation and preferred contact information.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Quaerens understands that, at the beginning of the collaboration, the law firm will need to steer the evidence and paperwork requirements so the back office can process cases correctly and consistently. Once the process is agreed, Quaerens expects the handover workflow to become faster and more efficient.</p>

  <h1>12. Why the Partnership Works</h1>
  <p>The proposed relationship is complementary. Quaerens provides consumer outreach, intake, screening, administration and document organisation. The law firm provides regulated legal assessment, client-care procedures, legal advice and case progression.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Quaerens already has operational infrastructure and trained intake/admin support.</li>
    <li>Lead-generation channels and specialist web pages are already in place.</li>
    <li>Campaigns have been tested under a more difficult upfront-fee model.</li>
    <li>No-win-no-fee availability is expected to improve consumer trust and conversion.</li>
    <li>The business can hand over organised client packs instead of raw enquiries.</li>
    <li>Additional claim routes are ready to launch and scale once legal progression is available.</li>
  </ul>

  <h1>13. Notes and Assumptions</h1>
  <ul>
    <li>This document is a management forecast and operating explanation, not filed statutory accounts.</li>
    <li>Lead volumes and conversion rates are management estimates based on current and previous campaign experience.</li>
    <li>Legal partner criteria will determine which clients can be accepted for review or progression.</li>
    <li>Any referral, marketing, administration or support arrangement should be checked against SRA, FCA, data protection and other relevant regulatory requirements.</li>
    <li>Additional staffing may be required as lead volumes increase.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="footer">Quaerens Ltd - Revised 12-Month P&amp;L and Operating Forecast - Draft for Discussion</div>
</body>
</html>
